Thetryblock contains the code that might throw an error.

If no error occurs, the catch block is skipped.

Thecatchblock executes only if an error occurs in the try block.

The error object provides details about what went wrong.

Thefinallyblock executes after the try and catch blocks,
whether an error occurred or not.

It is commonly used for cleanup tasks (e.g., closing files, stopping loaders, etc.).

When an error occurs, JavaScript will 
normally stop and generate an error message.

The technical term for this is: JavaScript willthrow an 
exception (throw an error).

Thethrowstatement allows you to create a custom error.

